<div class="blocks"><div class="row split"><div class="block media"><p><img alt="" data-nw="1920" decoding="async" height="366" loading="lazy" src="/images/science-cafe/science-cafe-31-august-2021.jpg" width="650"/></p></div><div class="block"><p class="short">31 AUGUST 2021</p><p>KITION PLANETARIUM &amp; OBSERVATORY</p><p>20:00 "ALL SKY CAMERAS AND TRIANGULATING OF METEORS AND NOCTILUCENT CLOUDS" ONLINE SCIENCE CAFÉ</p><p>Guest speaker: Prof. David Rees (UK)</p><p class="short"><a href="https://us02web.zoom.us/j/83123362644?pwd=MW8zdTJmckU3Q2lnZE5pbGl1YjFFQT09&amp;fbclid=IwAR0OOVjSz2fhlG_sPjMH71k1OGwjoxf-MgvFyylk5xFV0JehABeLv7EuyWY#success" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" title="KITION ZOOM MEETING">Join Zoom Meeting</a></p><dl class="specs"><dt>Meeting ID</dt><dd> 831 2336 2644</dd><dt>Passcode</dt><dd> kition</dd></dl><p>Since 2008 KITION PLANETARIUM &amp; OBSERVATORY has offered Science Café sessions for all visitors to share their passion for science and astronomy.</p><p>From the year 2021 we are holding Science Café meetings online, as well.</p><p>Since January, visitors from different parts of the world participated in our online sessions, discussing observations, latest news in astronomy and sharing their achievements.</p><p>Following very many attempts (and failures!), the first successful measurements of Stellar Parallax were made as recently as the late 1830s, providing real information on the distances of stars. The thread of this talk will be to follow the major technological steps that have led from “Stellar Parallax” to the current generation of “All-Sky Cameras”, related devices and their many applications. </p><p>Along with powerful computer analysis techniques, very affordable instruments are now available  for a number of important astronomical and geophysical techniques.</p><p>For example, the precise triangulation of the height and orbits of meteor / meteoroid trails,  determination of the height and structure of Noctilucent Clouds, the accurate tracking of scientific experiments  using rocket-borne chemical releases and the precise tracking of the re-entry of major satellites, </p></div></div></div>
<p>such as ATV-1 (ESA / NASA - Jules Verne). Practical examples of each of these will be provided. </p>
<p>The talk will also cover currently available image sensors that provide very high sensitivity / low noise /fast read-out speeds,  enhancing the performance of these instruments.</p>
<p>Free entrance.</p>
